===Obstruents===
{{More citations needed section|date=March 2017}}
* The [[glottal stop]] {{IPA|[ʔ]}} is not a [[phoneme]] because it occurs only optionally in a few specific predictable environments:
** It always separates {{IPA|/aː, ə/}} and other vowels.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=75}}
** It often occurs at the beginning of vowel-initial words after a pause.
*** Moreso when separate vowel articulations are present within words (e.g. ''IQ-onderzoek'', ''milieu-imago'', ''toe-eigenen'', ''coöperatie'', and ''beaam''). And even more common in the case of compounds where both elements have the same vowel at the syllable-boundary (e.g. ''hippie-ideaal'', ''mee-eten'', ''glijijzer'', ''bui-uitdoving'', ''kilo-ohm'', ''trouwauto'').
* Stops:
** Voiceless: Unlike in English and German, Dutch's are [[unaspirated]] word initially: both English ''tip'' {{Audio|en-us-tip.ogg|}} and German {{lang|de|Tipp}} {{Audio|De-Tipp.ogg|}} are {{IPA|[tʰɪp]}}, but Dutch {{lang|nl|tip}} {{Audio|Nl-tip.ogg|}} is {{IPA|[tɪp]}} with an unaspirated {{IPA|[t]}}.
** Voiced:
*** {{IPA|/b/}} and {{IPA|/d/}} are fully voiced.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=75}}
*** {{IPA|/ɡ/}} is not a native phoneme of Dutch and occurs only in loanwords like ''goal'' ('goal') but is analyzed as a phoneme because minimal pairs exist: ''goal'' {{IPA|/ɡoːl/}} and ''kool'' {{IPA|/koːl/}} ('cabbage'). Additionally, in native words, {{IPA|[ɡ]}} occurs as an allophone of {{IPA|/k/}} when it undergoes regressive voicing assimilation like in ''zakdoek'' {{IPA|[ˈzɑɡduk]}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=205}}
** /Pj/:
*** In [[Syllable#Onset|onset]]s, the sequence {{IPA|/tj/}} is commonly realised as a [[Tenuis consonant|tenuis]] [[Voiceless alveolo-palatal affricate|alveolo-palatal affricate]] {{IPA|[t͡ɕ]}},{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=193}} or intervocalically as a stop {{IPAblink|c}} or fricative {{IPAblink|ç}}, barring some loanwords and names.
*** In [[Syllable#Onset|onset]]s, the sequence {{IPA|/dj/}} is commonly realised as a [[Tenuis consonant|tenuis]] [[Voiced alveolo-palatal affricate|alveolo-palatal affricate]] {{IPA|[d͡ʑ]}},{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=171}} or intervocalically as a stop {{IPAblink|ɟ}} or fricative {{IPAblink|ʝ}}, barring some loanwords and names.
*** The sequence {{IPA|/kj/}} is often realised as a [[voiceless palatal affricate|voiceless post-palatal affricate]] {{IPA|[c̠͡ç̠]}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=193}}
* Fricatives:
** Voicing:
*** In the Netherlands, {{IPA|/v/}} can devoice and merge with {{IPA|/f/}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=74}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=48}} According to {{Harvcoltxt|Collins|Mees|2003}}, hardly any speakers of Northern Standard Dutch consistently contrast {{IPA|/v/}} with {{IPA|/f/}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=48}}
*** In low-prestige varieties of Netherlandic Dutch (such as the Amsterdam accent){{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=48}} {{IPA|/z/}} can also devoice and merge with {{IPA|/s/}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=74}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=48}}
*** Speakers who devoice {{IPA|/v/}} and {{IPA|/z/}} may also hypercorrectively voice {{IPA|/f/}} and {{IPA|/s/}}: ''concert'' "concert" may thus be {{IPA|[kɔnˈzɛrt]}} compared to the more usual {{IPA|[kɔnˈsɛrt]}}.
*** Some speakers pronounce {{IPA|/ɦ/}} as a voiceless {{IPAblink|h}}.
** Coronal:
*** In the Netherlands, {{IPA|/s/}} and {{IPA|/z/}} may have only mid-to-low pitched friction, and for many Netherlandic speakers, they are [[Voiceless alveolar fricative#Voiceless alveolar retracted sibilant|retracted]]. In Belgium, they are more similar to English {{IPA|/s, z/}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=75}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=190}}
*** The sequences {{IPA|/sj/}} and {{IPA|/zj/}} are often assimilated to palatalised {{IPA|[sʲ, zʲ]}}, [[alveolo-palatal]] {{IPA|[ɕ, ʑ]}}, [[postalveolar]] {{IPA|[ʃ, ʒ]}} or similar realisations.
*** {{IPA|[ʃ, ʒ]}} are not native phonemes of Dutch and usually occur only in borrowed words like ''show'' and ''bagage'' "baggage". Depending on the speaker and the position in the word, they may or may not be distinct from the assimilated realisations of the clusters {{IPA|/sj, zj/}}. If they are not distinct, they have the same range of realisations as noted above.
** Dorsal
*** In the north, {{IPA|/ɣ/}} is often realised the same as {{IPA|/x/}}; the quality of that merged sound has been variously described as a voiceless post-velar {{IPAblink|x̠}} or uvular {{IPAblink|χ}} fricative.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=74}} Popularly, this is called [[Hard and soft G in Dutch|''harde g'']] {{gloss|hard g}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=191}} Although they share a common phone, the two phonemes aren't said to have merged as the past tense suffix, whose voicing status is determined by the previous consonant's, still bases itself on the underlying phonemes, e.g. {{wikt-lang|nl|logen}} + -{{abbr|''D''|Either t or d.}}''e'' > ''loogde'' {{gloss|leached (sg.)}} but {{wikt-lang|nl|lachen}} + -{{abbr|''D''|Either t or d.}}''e'' > ''lachte'' {{gloss|laughed (sg.)}}.{{sfnp|Taalportaal|2015|loc=§Further discussion}}
*** In the south, the distinction between {{IPA|/x/}} and {{IPA|/ɣ/}} is generally preserved as velar {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|x}}, {{IPAplink|ɣ}}]}} or post-palatal {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|x̟}}, {{IPAplink|ɣ˖}}]}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=74}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|pp=243, 245}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=191–192}} Some southern speakers may alternate between the velar and post-palatal articulation, depending on the backness of the preceding or succeeding vowel. Often referred to as [[Hard and soft G in Dutch|''zachte g'']] {{gloss|soft g}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=191}} 
*** In [[Zeeland]] and West Flanders, there is also a third variant, called [[Hard and soft G in Dutch|''zwakke harde g'']] {{gloss|weak hard g}}, in which {{IPA|/ɣ/}} is realised as {{IPAblink|ɦ}} and {{IPA|/x/}} is realised as {{IPAblink|h}}. They are [[h-dropping]] areas, so {{IPA|/ɦ/}} does not merge with glottal variants of {{IPA|/ɣ/}} and {{IPA|/x/}}.{{Citation needed|date=January 2024}}
*** Some dialects, particularly those from the southwest, exhibit [[h-dropping]].

===Sonorants===
* Nasals assimilate in the following manner. This also occurs across word-boundaries in [[allegro speech]] and may not happen word internally when overenunciating:
** {{IPA|/m/}} and {{IPA|/n/}} to a following labial [[obstruent]]:
*** before bilabials {{IPA|/p, b/}} they merge into {{IPA|[m]}}.
*** before labio-dentals {{IPA|/f, v/}} they merge into {{IPA|[ɱ]}}.
** {{IPA|/n/}} to a following [[dorsal consonant]]s:
*** before velars {{IPA|/k, ɡ, x, ɣ/}}, it merges with {{IPA|/ŋ/}}. 
****The realisation of {{IPA|/ŋ/}} in turn depends on how a following velar fricative is realised. For example, it is uvular {{IPA|[ɴ]}} for speakers who realise {{IPA|/x, ɣ/}} as uvulars.
*** before palatals, {{IPA|/j/}}, {{IPA|[ɕ, ʑ]}} -assimilated {{IPA|/sj, zj/}} and loan introduced-, it is realised as {{IPA|[ɲ]}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=75}}
* {{IPAslink|ʋ}} varies mostly according to regional dialect:
** In central and northern Netherlands, it is a labiodental approximant {{IPAblink|ʋ}} or even a voiced labiodental fricative {{IPAblink|v}}.{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=8}}
** Speakers in southern Netherlands and Belgium (also Hasselt and Maastricht dialects)  use a [[voiced bilabial fricative|bilabial approximant]] {{IPAblink|β̞}}.{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=8}} It is like {{IPAblink|w}} but without [[velarization|velarisation]].
*** {{IPAblink|ɥ}} may join the allophony.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=198}}
** In [[Suriname]] and among immigrant populations, {{IPAblink|w}} is common.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=199}}{{clarify|date=March 2017}}
* The exact pronunciation of {{IPA|/l/}} varies regionally:{{anchor|Dutch /l/}}
** In the North, {{IPA|/l/}} is 'clear' before vowels and 'dark' before consonants and pauses. Intervocalic {{IPA|/l/}} tends to be clear except after the open back vowels {{IPA|/ɔ, ɑ/}}. However, some speakers use the dark variant in all intervocalic contexts.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=197}}
** Some accents, such as in Amsterdam and Rotterdam, have dark {{IPA|/l/}} in all positions. Conversely, some accents in the eastern regions, along the German border (for example around [[Nijmegen]]), as well as some speakers of Standard Belgian, have clear {{IPA|/l/}} in all contexts.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=197}}
** The quality of dark {{IPA|[ɫ]}} varies:
*** In the North, it is [[pharyngealization|pharyngealised]] {{IPA|[lˤ]}}, e.g. {{Audio-IPA|Nl-zal.ogg|[zɑlˤ]}}. 
**** In a final position, many of such speakers produce a strongly pharyngealised vocoid with no alveolar contact ({{IPA|[ɤˤ]}}) instead, {{nowrap|e.g. {{Audio-IPA|Nl-schandaal.ogg|[sχɑnˈdaːɤ̯ˤ]}}}}.
*** In Belgium, it is either [[velarization|velarised]] {{IPA|[lˠ]}}, e.g. {{Audio-IPA|Nl-schelp (Belgium).ogg|[sxɛlˠp]}}; or post-palatalised {{IPA|[lʲ̠]}},{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=58, 197, 222}} e.g. {{Audio-IPA|Nl-schandaal (Belgium).ogg|[sxanˈdaːlʲ̠]}}.
** Palatal {{IPAblink|ʎ}} may appear before {{IPA|/j/}}.{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}
* {{IPA|/r/}} varies considerably from dialect to dialect and even between speakers in the same dialect area:{{anchor|Dutch /r/}}
** Coronal realisations are the historical pronunciation of the phoneme, spearheaded by the [[alveolar trill]] {{IPAblink|r}} and the [[alveolar tap]] {{IPAblink|ɾ}} as common allophone.
*** There is, in fact, a considerable amount of speakers who never realise alveolar /r/ as a trill, although most have at least some trills. At most, a few speakers were observed to have over 50% of their alveolar realisations as trills.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=142}}
*** Fricative realisation can also nowadays be heard: [{{IPA link|ɹ̝̊}}~{{IPA link|ɹ̝}}], authors do not say where exactly it is used;{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=199}} word finally and before /s/, {{IPAblink|r̝̊}} is not uncommon,{{Citation needed|date=April 2025}} e.g. {{Audio-IPA|Nl-blauwverschuiving_(Belgium).ogg|[...vəˌr̝̊s̠xœʏ̯.vɪŋ]}}
** Uvular continuants are rising in popularity, the [[uvular trill]] {{IPAblink|ʀ}} serving as flagship. It is found particularly in the central and southern dialect areas and increasingly in the Randstad.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=209}} e.g. {{Audio-IPA|Nl-rad.ogg|[ʀɑt]}}. 
*** There is only a small number of uvular r speakers without at least some trills. There are also speakers who always trill. Many speakers, of course, are in between the two extremes, and mix trilled realisations with non-trilled ones (typically approximant {{IPAblink|ʁ̞}}) in all possible proportions.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=147}} In prevocalic context, most uvular speakers have at least a few fricative realisation, but very few people have very many of them (although for speakers who have very many of them, they are almost general). {{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=166}} In codas, the uvular fricative realisations (typically voiceless) take the lead, particularly in the Belgian Dutch accents. But they are very rare in the Northern ones.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=167}}
*** The coastal dialects of South Holland produce a [[voiced uvular fricative]] {{IPAblink|ʁ}}.
*** In Hague and Leiden, {{IPAblink|ʀ}} and {{IPAblink|ʁ̞}} remain the 2 most common rhotics in onset positions (with the former being more frequent). However, the Uvular fricatives {{IPAblink|χ}} or {{IPAblink|ʁ}} are not uncommon, though they are rare in other Randstad cities (especially Amsterdam). Alveolar realizations are practically non-existent in The Hague, while it's a noticeable minority in Leiden. In the post-vocalic positions, the [[velar bunched approximant]] {{IPAblink|ɹ̈}} is overwhelmingly common in both cities.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=110-115, 115-120}}
*** In Rotterdam, there is a half-half split between {{IPAblink|ɾ}} and {{IPAblink|ʀ}}, with some speakers mixing both. {{IPAblink|ʀ}} is somewhat more frequent than {{IPAblink|ʁ̞}} for Uvular speakers, fricative realizations are relatively rare. {{IPAblink|ɹ̈}} remains the most frequent one in post-vocalic positions, for both Uvular and alveolar users.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=102-107}}
***In Utrecht, the uvular realizations {{IPAblink|ʁ̞}} and {{IPAblink|ʀ}} dominate the onset positions (the former being more frequent, parallel to Amsterdam). The alveolar realizations are present but are a minority. {{IPAblink|ɹ̈}} overwhelmingly dominates the post-vocalic positions, just like the other cities.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=107-110}}
*** In Amsterdam, as well as in Flanders, the [[Voiced uvular fricative|voiced uvular approximant]] {{IPAblink|ʁ̞}} is a minority.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=194}}
*** Alveolar realizations form the majority of onsets in Amsterdam, the most frequent variant among them is the [[alveolar tap]] {{IPAblink|ɾ}}. However, uvular variants are increasing, particularly among younger speakers. The most frequent among the uvular variants are [[uvular approximant]] {{IPAblink|ʁ̞}} and [[uvular trill]] {{IPAblink|ʀ}}, the approximant is slightly more frequent. Uvular fricatives are quite rare. Approximately 62.5% of the people use alveolar, 20% uvular and 17.5% mix both. In codas, {{IPAblink|ɹ̈}} is the most frequent.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=99-102}}
*** In most of the Flanders, especially Bruges and Antwerp, the /r/ is alveolar. In onsets, the most common alveolar variant is [[voiced alveolar tap]] {{IPAblink|ɾ}} and to a lesser extent, [[voiced alveolar trill]] {{IPAblink|r}}. In codas, the [[voiceless alveolar fricative trill]] {{IPAblink|r̝̊}} remains the most common.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=86-89, 89-93}}
*** In some flemish cities, particularly in Ghent, Hasselt, Brussels etc, the uvular variants form the majority. Though alveolar users are a significant minority, forming around 35% of speakers in Hasselt. In onsets, the [[uvular approximant]] {{IPAblink|ʁ̞}} and [[uvular trill]] {{IPAblink|ʀ}} remain the most frequent. In codas, [[voiceless uvular fricative trill]] {{IPAblink|ʀ̝̊}} and [[voiceless uvular fricative]] {{IPAblink|χ}} are frequent.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=93-96, 96-99}}
*** In Nijmegen, uvular /r/ is majority. Alveolar realizations are very rare. The most frequent in onsets is the [[uvular approximant]] {{IPAblink|ʁ̞}}. [[Uvular trill]] {{IPAblink|ʀ}}, [[uvular fricative trill]] {{IPAblink|ʀ̝}} and [[uvular fricative]] {{IPAblink|ʁ}} also occur but not as frequent as the approximant. There are various common realizations in coda, they include the [[uvular approximant]] {{IPAblink|ʁ̞}}, [[velar bunched approximant]] {{IPAblink|ɹ̈}}, [[schwa]] {{IPAblink|ə}} and [[voiceless uvular fricative]] {{IPAblink|χ}}. Coda r-elision isn't uncommon. Most speakers use multiple coda realizations.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=120-123}}
*** Syllable-finally, they may be vocalised to {{IPAblink|ɐ}}, much as in German, which is more common in the (south)eastern areas ([[Limburg (Netherlands)|Limburg]], southeast Brabantian, [[Overijssel]]), e.g. {{Audio-IPA|Nl-scheur weg.ogg|[sχøəɐ̯...]}}.
** The [[uvular trill]] {{IPAblink|ʀ}} can also be found within the [[Dutch Low Saxon]] dialect area, where it is attested in the cities of [[Zutphen]], [[Steenwijk]],<ref>[http://www.detaalvanoverijssel.nl/plaats/id:6 De Taal van Overijssel]. Over de taal van Steenwijk.</ref> [[Kampen, Overijssel|Kampen]],<ref>[http://www.detaalvanoverijssel.nl/plaats/id:18 De Taal van Overijssel]. Over de taal van Kampen.</ref> [[Zwolle]]<ref>[http://www.detaalvanoverijssel.nl/plaats/id:19 De Taal van Overijssel]. Over de taal van Zwolle.</ref> and [[Deventer]].<ref>[http://www.detaalvanoverijssel.nl/plaats/id:34 De Taal van Overijssel]. Over de taal van Deventer</ref> In [[IJsselmuiden]], a locality situated near [[Kampen]], it can also be heard.<ref>Ph Bloemhoff-de Bruijn, Anderhalve Eeuw Zwols Vocaalveranderingsprocessen in de periode 1838–1972. IJsselacademie (2012). {{ISBN|978-90-6697-228-5}}</ref> In rural areas of the [[Low Saxon]] region, the [[alveolar trill]] {{IPAblink|r}} remains the prevailing realization of /r/.<ref>Ph Bloemhoff-de Bruijn, Anderhalve Eeuw Zwols Vocaalveranderingsprocessen in de periode 1838–1972. IJsselacademie (2012). {{ISBN|978-90-6697-228-5}}</ref>
** The [[velar bunched approximant]] {{IPA|[ɹ̈]}} (the ''Gooise R'', which sounds similar to the [[retroflex approximant]]) is found at the [[Syllable#Coda|end of a syllable]] in the pronunciation of some speakers in the Netherlands, especially those from the [[Randstad]], but not in Belgium<!--Brugse kinderen, Wikipedia's bane-->. Its use has been increasing in recent years.{{sfnp|Sebregts|2014|pp=196–198}} E.g. {{Audio-IPA|Nl-Nederlanders.ogg|[ˈneɪ.dəɹ̈ˌlɑn.dəɹθ̠]}}.
** Other variants include {{IPAblink|ɽ͡r}}{{sfnp|Goeman|Van de Velde|2001|p=107}} in North Brabant and North Holland.

An [[epenthesis|epenthetic]] {{IPAblink|ə}} may be inserted between {{IPA|/l, r/}} and word-final {{IPA|/m, n, p, k, f, x/}}. Thus ''melk'' {{IPA|/mɛlk/}} "milk" may be pronounced {{IPA|[ˈmɛlək]}}. That may extend to compounds: ''melkboer'' {{IPA|[ˈmɛləkbuːr]}} "milkman". Although this pronunciation is mistakenly thought of as non-standard, it is found in all types of Dutch, including the standard varieties. There is also another type of {{IPA|[ə]}}-insertion that occurs word-medially (e.g. ''helpen'' {{IPA|[ˈɦɛləpə]}} "to help"), which is considered non-standard.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=197–198, 201}}

In many areas, the final 'n' of the ending ''-en'' (originally {{IPA|/ən/}}, with a variety of meanings) is pronounced only if a word is being individually stressed, which makes ''-en'' words [[homophone|homophonous]] with otherwise-identical forms ending in ''-e'' alone. The ''-n'' is dropped both word-finally and, in compound words, word-internally. This pronunciation can be morphologically sensitive and serve to distinguish words since the ''-n'' is dropped only when it is part of the distinct ending ''-en'', not when it is in a word that has an indivisible stem that happens to end in ''-en''. Thus, the ''teken'' of ''ik teken'' ('I draw') always retains its ''-n'' because it is part of an indivisible stem, but ''teken'' ('ticks') drops it since it is part of a plural ending. Such pairs (''teken'' = 'draw'; ''teken'' = 'ticks'), despite being written identically, are therefore not homophones in dialects that drop ''-n''.

Final ''-n'' is retained in the North East ([[Low Saxon]]) and the South West (East and West Flemish), where it is the schwa that disappears instead. This creates a syllabic {{IPA|[n]}} or (after velars) syllabic {{IPA|[ŋ]}} sounds: ''laten'' {{IPA|[ˈlaːtn̩]}}; ''maken'' {{IPA|[ˈmaːkŋ̍]}}. Some Low Saxon dialects that have uvular pronunciations of {{IPA|/ɣ/}} and {{IPA|/x/}} (or one of them) also have a syllabic [[uvular nasal]], as in ''lagen'' and/or ''lachen'' {{IPA|[ˈlaːχɴ̩]}}

===Final devoicing and assimilation===
Dutch [[Final-obstruent devoicing|devoices]] all [[obstruents]] at the ends of words, which is partly reflected in the spelling. The voiced "z" in plural {{lang|nl|hui'''z'''en}} {{IPA|[ˈɦœy̑zə(n)]}} becomes {{lang|nl|hui'''s'''}} {{IPA|[ɦœy̑s]}} ('house') in singular. Also, {{lang|nl|dui'''v'''en}} {{IPA|[ˈdœy̑və(n)]}} becomes {{lang|nl|dui'''f'''}} {{IPA|[dœy̑f]}} ('dove'). The other cases are always written with the voiced consonant, but a devoiced one is actually pronounced: the "d" in plural {{lang|nl|baar'''d'''en}} {{IPA|[ˈbaːrdə(n)]}} is retained in singular spelling {{lang|nl|baar'''d'''}} ('beard'), but the pronunciation of the latter is {{IPA|[baːrt]}}, and plural {{lang|nl|ri'''bb'''en}} {{IPA|[ˈrɪbə(n)]}} has singular {{lang|nl|ri'''b'''}} ('rib'), pronounced {{IPA|[rɪp]}}.
{{Listen
| filename = Nl-huis.ogg
