[[Image:Belgian Dutch monophthongs chart.svg|thumb|upright=1.13|Monophthongs of Belgian Standard Dutch, from {{Harvcoltxt|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}. The schwa {{IPA|/ə/|cat=no}} is not shown.|class=skin-invert-image]]
[[Image:Dutch allophones of unrounded monophthongs.svg|thumb|upright=1.13|Dutch allophones of unrounded monophthongs, from {{Harvcoltxt|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=92, 130, 132, 134}}. Black vowels occur before {{IPA|/r/}} in Northern Standard Dutch and Randstad Dutch, and the red vowel occurs before the dark {{IPA|/l/|cat=no}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=92, 130, 132, 234}}|class=skin-invert-image]]
[[Image:Dutch allophones of rounded monophthongs.svg|thumb|upright=1.13|Dutch allophones of rounded monophthongs, from {{Harvcoltxt|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=98, 130, 132, 134}}. Black vowels occur before {{IPA|/r/}} in Northern Standard Dutch and Randstad Dutch, and the blue vowel occurs before {{IPA|/ŋ/|cat=no}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=98, 130, 132, 234}}|class=skin-invert-image]]

* Dutch vowels can be classified as lax and tense,<ref>For example by {{Harvb|Gussenhoven|1999|p=75}}.</ref> checked and free<ref>For example by {{Harvb|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=127–128, 132–133}}.</ref> or short and long.<ref>For example by {{Harvtxt|Booij|1999|pp=4–5}} and {{Harvb|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}.</ref> Phonetically however, the close vowels {{IPA|/i, y, u/}} are as short as the phonological lax/short vowels unless they occur before {{IPA|/r/}}.{{sfnp|Booij|1999|pp=4–6}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=132}}
* Phonologically, {{IPA|/ɪ, ʏ, ʊ/}} can be classified as either close or close-mid. [[Carlos Gussenhoven]] classifies them as the former,{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|2007|pp=337, 339}} whereas [[Geert Booij]] says that they are the latter and classifies {{IPA|/ɛ, ɔ/}} and the non-native mid vowels as open-mid.{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=5}}
* {{IPA|/ʏ/}} has been traditionally transcribed with {{angbr IPA|œ}}, but modern sources tend to use {{angbr IPA|ʏ}} or {{angbr IPA|ɵ}} instead.{{sfnp|van Heuven|Genet|2002}}<ref>Sources that use {{angbr IPA|ʏ}} include {{Harvtxt|Booij|1999|pp=4–5}}, {{Harvtxt|Gussenhoven|1999|pp=75–76}} and {{Harvtxt|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}. The online dictionary [http://woorden.org woorden.org] also uses that symbol. Sources that use {{angbr IPA|ɵ}} include {{Harvtxt|van Reenen|Elias|1998}} and {{Harvtxt|Rietveld|van Heuven|2009}}. The traditional transcription of {{angbr IPA|œ}} is also used in certain modern sources, for example by {{Harvb|Kooij|van Oostendorp|2003|p=27}}.</ref> Beverley Collins and Inger Mees write this vowel with {{angbr IPA|ʉ}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=128}}
* The phonemic status of {{IPA|/ʊ/}} is not clear. Phonetically, a vowel of the {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ʊ|ʊ̞}} ~ {{IPAplink|ɔ̝|ɔ̽}}]}}<ref>Described as close-mid {{IPAblink|ʊ|ʊ̞}} by {{harvb|Booij|1999}} and as mid {{IPAblink|ɔ̝|ɔ̽}} by {{harvb|Collins|Mees|2003}}.</ref> type appears before nasals as an allophone of {{IPA|/ɔ/}}, e.g. in ''jong'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-jong.ogg|[jʊŋ]}} ('young'). This vowel can also be found in certain other words, such as ''op'' {{IPA|[ʊp]}} ('on'), which can form a near-minimal pair with ''mop'' {{IPA|[mɔp]}} ('joke'). This, however, is subject to both individual and geographical variation,{{sfnp|Booij|1999|pp=7, 17}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=97–98}} compare ''song'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-song.ogg|[sʷɔɔ̯̃ŋ]}} ~ ''gong'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-gong.ogg|[ɣ̊oɰ̃ŋ]}} ~ ''dong'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-dong.ogg|[dʊ̃ŋ]}} from the same speaker.
* Many speakers feel that {{IPA|/ə/}} and {{IPA|/ʏ/}} belong to the same phoneme, with {{IPAblink|ə}} being its unstressed variant. This is reflected in spelling errors produced by Dutch children, for example {{angbr|binnu}} for ''binnen'' {{IPA|[ˈbɪnə(n)]}} ('inside'). Adding to this, the two vowels have different phonological distribution; for example, {{IPA|/ə/}} can occur word-finally, while {{IPA|/ʏ/}} (along with other lax vowels) cannot. In addition, the word-final allophone of {{IPA|/ə/}} is a close-mid front vowel with some rounding {{IPAblink|ø|ø̜}}, a sound that is similar to {{IPA|/ʏ/}}.{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=5}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=129}}
* The native tense vowels {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː, aː/}} are long {{IPA|[eː, øː, oː, aː]}} in stressed syllables and short {{IPA|[e, ø, o, a]}} elsewhere. The non-native oral vowels appear only in stressed syllables and thus are always long.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|2007|pp=342, 344}}
* The native {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː, aː/}} as well as the non-native nasal {{IPA|/ɛ̃ː, œ̃ː, ɔ̃ː, ɑ̃ː/}} are sometimes transcribed without the length marks, as {{angbr IPA|e, ø, o, a, ɛ̃, œ̃, ɔ̃, ɑ̃}}.<ref>For example by {{Harvtxt|Booij|1999}} and {{Harvtxt|Heemskerk|Zonneveld|2000}} as well as the online dictionary [http://www.woorden.org woorden.org].</ref>
* The non-native {{IPA|/iː, yː, uː, ɛː, œː, ɔː/}} occur only in stressed syllables. In unstressed syllables, they are replaced by the closest native vowel. For instance, verbs corresponding to the nouns ''analyse'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-analyse.ogg|/aːnaːˈliːzə/|help=no}} ('analysis'), ''centrifuge'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-centrifuge.ogg|/sɛntriˈfyːzjə/|help=no}} ('spinner'), and ''zone'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-zone.ogg|/ˈzɔːnə/|help=no}} ('zone') are ''analyseren'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-analyseren.ogg|/aːnaːliˈzeːrən/|help=no}} ('to analyze'), ''centrifugeren'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-centrifugeren.ogg|/sɛntrifyˈɣeːrən/|help=no}} ('to spin-dry'), and ''zoneren'' {{IPA|/zoːˈneːrən/}} ('to divide into zones').{{sfnp|Booij|1999|pp=6, 16}}
* {{IPA|/œː/}} is extremely rare, and the only words of any frequency in which it occurs are ''oeuvre'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-oeuvre.ogg|/ˈœːvrə/|help=no}}, ''manoeuvre'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-manoeuvre.ogg|/maˈnœːvrə/|help=no}} and ''freule''. In the more common words, {{IPA|/ɛː/}} tends to be replaced with the native {{IPA|/ɛ/}}, whereas {{IPA|/ɔː/}} can be replaced by either {{IPA|/ɔ/}} or {{IPA|/oː/}} (Belgians typically select the latter).{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=137–138}}
* The non-native nasal vowels {{IPA|/ɛ̃ː, œ̃ː, ɔ̃ː, ɑ̃ː/}} occur only in loanwords from French.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=75}}{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=6}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=138}} {{IPA|/ɛ̃ː, ɔ̃ː, ɑ̃ː/}} are often nativised as {{IPA|/ɛn, ɔn, ɑn/}}, {{IPA|/ɛŋ, ɔŋ, ɑŋ/}} or {{IPA|/ɛm, ɔm, ɑm/}}, depending on the place of articulation of the following consonant. For instance, ''restaurant'' {{IPA|/rɛstoːˈrɑ̃ː/}} ('restaurant') and ''pardon'' {{IPA|/pɑrˈdɔ̃ː/}} ('excuse me') are often nativised as {{Audio-IPA|Nl-restaurant.ogg|/rɛstoːˈrɑnt/|help=no}} and {{Audio-IPA|Nl-pardon.ogg|/pɑrˈdɔn/|help=no}}, respectively.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=138}} {{IPA|/œ̃ː/}} is extremely rare, just like its oral counterpart,<ref name="some">It is listed by only some sources: {{Harvtxt|Booij|1999}} and {{Harvb|Gussenhoven|2007}}.</ref> and the only word of any frequency in which it occurs is ''parfum'' {{IPA|/pɑrˈfœ̃ː/}} ('perfume'), often nativised as {{IPA|/pɑrˈfʏm/}} or {{Audio-IPA|Nl-parfum.ogg|/ˈpɑrfʏm/|help=no}}.
* The non-native {{IPA|/ɑː/}} is listed only by some sources.<ref>Such as {{Harvtxt|Booij|1999}} and {{Harvb|Gussenhoven|2007}}.</ref> It occurs in words such as ''cast'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-cast.ogg|/kɑːst/|help=no}} ('cast').{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=6}}{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|2007|p=342}}

The following sections describe the phonetic quality of Dutch [[monophthongs]] in detail.

====Close vowels====
* {{IPA|/ɪ/}} is near the canonical value of the IPA symbol {{angbr IPA|ɪ}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=128}} The Standard Belgian realisation has also been described as close-mid {{IPAblink|ɪ|ɪ̞}}.{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}} In regional Standard Dutch, the realisation may be different: for example, in Antwerp it is closer, more like {{IPAblink|i}}, whereas in places like Dordrecht, Nijmegen, West and East Flanders the vowel is typically more open than the Standard Dutch counterpart, more like {{IPAblink|e|ë}}. Affected speakers of Northern Standard Dutch may also use this vowel.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=131}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=246}}
* {{IPA|/i, iː/}} are close front {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|i}}, {{IPAplink|iː}}]}}, close to cardinal {{IPAblink|i}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=132}}{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}
* The majority of sources consider {{IPA|/ʏ/}} to be close-mid central {{IPAblink|ɵ}},{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}<ref name="vanheuvengenet">{{Harvtxt|van Heuven|Genet|2002}}, cited in {{Harvb|Gussenhoven|2007|pp=337–338}}.</ref>{{sfnp|Rietveld|van Heuven|2009}} yet Beverley Collins and Inger Mees consider it to be close-mid front {{IPAblink|ʏ|ʏ̞}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=128}} The study conducted by Vincent van Heuven and Roos Genet has shown that native speakers consider the canonical IPA value of the symbol {{angbr IPA|ɵ}} to be the most similar one to the Dutch sound, much more than the canonical values of {{angbr IPA|ʏ}} and {{angbr IPA|œ}} (the sound represented by {{angbr IPA|ʉ}} was not a part of the study).<ref name="vanheuvengenet"/> In regional Standard Dutch {{IPA|/ʏ/}} may be raised to near-close {{IPAblink|ɵ̝}}, for example in Amsterdam, Rotterdam and The Hague. In Antwerp, the vowel may be as high as {{IPA|/y/}} and the two vowels may differ in nothing but length. A more open vowel of the {{IPAblink|ɵ̞}}-type is found in southern accents (e.g. in Bruges) and in affected Northern Standard Dutch.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=131}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=246}}
* {{IPA|/y, yː/}} have been variously described as close front {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|y}}, {{IPAplink|yː}}]}},{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|2007|p=338}} near-close front {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|y˕}}, {{IPAplink|y˕ː}}]}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=132}} and, in Northern Standard Dutch, near-close central {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ʉ̞}}, {{IPAplink|ʉ̞ː}}]}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}
* {{IPA|/u, uː/}} are close back {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|u}}, {{IPAplink|uː}}]}} in Northern Standard Dutch and close near-back {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|u|u̟}}, {{IPAplink|u|u̟ː}}]}} in Belgian Standard Dutch and some varieties of regional Standard Dutch spoken in Antwerp and Flemish Brabant.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=132–133}}

Word-final {{IPA|/i, y, u/}} are raised and end in a voiceless vowel: {{IPA|[ii̥, yẙ, uu̥]}}. The voiceless vowel in the first sequence may sound almost like a palatal fricative {{IPAblink|ç}},{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=132}} e.g. {{Audio|Nl-drie.ogg|drie}} ('three'), {{Audio|Nl-q.ogg|"q"}}, {{Audio|Nl-toe.ogg|toe}} ('till {{abbr|adv.|adverbial}}').

{{IPA|/i, y, u/}} are frequently longer in Belgian Standard Dutch and most Belgian accents than in Northern Standard Dutch in which the length of those vowels is identical to that of lax vowels.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=132}}

Regardless of the exact accent, {{IPA|/i, y, u/}} are always lengthened to {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|iː}}, {{IPAplink|yː}}, {{IPAplink|uː}}]}} before {{IPA|/r/}} in the same word.{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=6}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=132}}{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}} In Northern Standard Dutch and in the Randstad, they are laxed to {{IPA|[i̽ː, y˕ː, u̽ː]}} and often have a schwa-like off-glide ({{IPA|[i̽ə, y˕ə, u̽ə]}}). This means that before {{IPA|/r/}}, {{IPA|/i, y, u/}} are less strongly differentiated from {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} in Northern Standard Dutch and in the Randstad than is usually the case in other regional varieties of Standard Dutch and in Belgian Standard Dutch.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=132, 134, 200}} There is one exception to the lengthening rule: when {{IPA|/r/}} is followed by a consonant different than {{IPA|/t/}} and {{IPA|/s/}}, {{IPA|/i, y, u/}} remain short. Examples of that are words such as ''wierp'' {{IPA|[ʋirp]}}, ''[[Duisburg]]'' {{IPA|[ˈdyzbur(ə)k]}} (alternatively: {{IPA|[ˈdœyzbʏr(ə)x]}}, with a lax vowel) and ''stierf'' {{IPA|[stirf]}}. The rule is also suppressed syllable-finally in certain compounds: compare ''roux-room'' {{IPA|[ˈruroːm]}} with ''roerroom'' {{IPA|[ˈruːr(r)oːm]}} and ''Ruhr-Ohm'' {{IPA|[ˈruːroːm]}}.{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=6}}{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|2007|pp=344, 347}}

====Mid vowels====
* {{IPA|/ɛ, ɛː/}} are open-mid front {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ɛ}}, {{IPAplink|ɛː}}]}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=128, 137}} According to Jo Verhoeven, the Belgian Standard Dutch variants are somewhat raised.{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}} Before {{IPA|/n/}} and the velarised or pharyngealised allophone of {{IPA|/l/}}, {{IPA|/ɛ/}} is typically lowered to {{IPAblink|æ}}. In some regional Standard Dutch (e.g. in Dordrecht, Ghent, Bruges and more generally in Zeeland, North Brabant and Limburg), the lowering is generalised to most or even all contexts. Conversely, some regional Standard Dutch varieties (such as much Randstad Dutch, especially the Amsterdam dialect, as well as the Antwerp accent) realise the main allophone of {{IPA|/ɛ/}} as higher and more central than open-mid front ({{IPAblink|ɛ̝|ɛ̝̈}}).{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=92, 128–129, 131}}
* {{IPA|/œː/}} is open-mid front {{IPAblink|œː}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=137}}
* {{IPA|/ə/}} has two allophones, with the main one being mid central unrounded {{IPAblink|ə}}. The allophone used in word-final positions resembles the main allophone of {{IPA|/ʏ/}} as it is closer, more front and more rounded ({{IPAblink|ø|ø̜}}).{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=129}}{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}
* {{IPA|/ɔ/}} is open-mid back rounded {{IPAblink|ɔ}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}} Collins and Mees (2003) describe it as "very tense", with [[pharyngealization|pharyngealisation]] and strong [[lip-rounding]].{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=132}} There is considerable regional and individual variation in the height of {{IPA|/ɔ/}}, with allophones being as close as {{IPAblink|ʊ}} in certain words.{{sfnp|Schouten|1981}}{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=7}} The closed allophones are especially common in the Randstad.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=132}} {{IPA|/ɔː/}} is near {{IPA|/ɔ/}} in terms of height and backness.

{{IPA|/ɛ, ɔ/}} are typically somewhat lengthened and centralised before {{IPA|/r/}} in Northern Standard Dutch and in the Randstad, usually with a slight schwa-like offglide: {{IPA|[ɛ̈ə̆, ɔ̈ə̆]}}. In addition, {{IPA|/ɔ/}} in this position is somewhat less rounded ({{IPA|[ɔ̜̈ə̆]}}) than the main allophone of {{IPA|/ɔ/}},{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=130}} e.g. ''sch'''or''''' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-schor.ogg|[sʷχɔ̜̈ə̯ʀ̥]}} ('hoarse'), ''st'''er''''' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-ster.ogg|[stɛ̈ə̆r̠]}} ('star')

The free vowels {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} are realised as monophthongs {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|eː}}, {{IPAplink|øː}}, {{IPAplink|oː}}]}} in Belgian Standard Dutch (Jo Verhoeven describes the Belgian Standard Dutch realisation of {{IPA|/øː/}} as mid-central {{IPAblink|ɵ̞ː}}) and in many regional accents. In Northern Standard Dutch, narrow closing diphthongs {{IPA|[eɪ, øʏ, oʊ]}} are used. The starting point of {{IPA|[oʊ]}} is centralised back ({{IPAblink|o|ö}}), and the starting point of {{IPA|[eɪ]}} has been described as front {{IPAblink|e}} by Collins and Mees and as centralised front {{IPAblink|e|ë}} by Gussenhoven. The monophthongal counterparts of {{IPA|[eɪ, oʊ]}} are peripheral; the former is almost as front as cardinal {{IPAblink|eː}}, whereas the latter is almost as back as cardinal {{IPAblink|oː}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=108, 110, 133–134}} Many speakers of Randstad Dutch and younger speakers of Northern Standard Dutch realise {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} as rather wide diphthongs of the {{IPA|[ɛɪ, œʏ, ɔʊ]}} type, which may be mistaken for the phonemic diphthongs {{IPA|/ɛi, œy, ɔu/}} by speakers of other accents.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=135}}{{sfnp|Jacobi|2009}} The use of {{IPA|[ɛɪ, œʏ, ɔʊ]}} for {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} goes hand in hand with the lowering the first elements of {{IPA|/ɛi, œy, ɔu/}} to {{IPA|[aɪ, aʏ, aʊ]}}, a phenomenon called ''[[Polder Dutch]]''. Therefore, the phonemic contrast between {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} and {{IPA|/ɛi, œy, ɔu/}} is still strongly maintained, but its phonetic realisation is very different from what can be typically heard in traditional Northern Standard Dutch.{{sfnp|Stroop|1999}} In Rotterdam and The Hague, the starting point of {{IPA|[oʊ]}} can be fronted to {{IPAblink|ə}}, instead of being lowered to {{IPAblink|ɔ}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=109–110}}

In Northern Standard Dutch and in the Randstad, {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} lose their closing glides and are raised and slightly centralised to {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ɪː}}, {{IPAplink|ʏː}}, {{IPAplink|ʊː}}]}} (often with a schwa-like off-glide {{IPA|[ɪə, ʏə, ʊə]}}) before {{IPA|/r/}} in the same word. The first two allophones strongly resemble the lax monophthongs {{IPA|/ɪ, ʏ/}}. Dutch children frequently misspell the word ''weer'' ('again') as ''wir''. Those sounds may also occur in regional varieties of Standard Dutch and in Belgian Standard Dutch, but they are more typically the same as the main allophones of {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} (that is, {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|eː}}, {{IPAplink|øː}}, {{IPAplink|oː}}]}}). An exception to the centralising rule are syllable-final {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} in compounds such as ''z'''ee'''reis'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-zeereis.ogg|[ˈzeɪˌrɛis]|help=no}} ('sea voyage'), ''mili'''eu'''ramp'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-milieuramp.ogg|[mɪlˈjøʏˌrɑmp]|help=no}} ('environmental disaster') and ''bur'''eau'''redactrice'' {{IPA|[byˈroʊredɑkˌtrisə]}} ('desk editor (f.)').{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=131, 134, 200–201}}{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|2007|pp=339, 347}}

In Northern Standard Dutch, {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} are mid-centralised before the pharyngealised allophone of {{IPA|/l/}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=134}}

Several non-standard dialects have retained the distinction between the so-called "sharp-long" and "soft-long" ''e'' and ''o'', which dates back to early [[Middle Dutch]]. The sharp-long varieties originate from the Old Dutch long ''ē'' and ''ō'' (Proto-Germanic ''ai'' and ''au''), and the soft-long varieties arose from short ''i''/''e'' and ''u''/''o'' that were [[open syllable lengthening|lengthened in open syllables]] in early Middle Dutch. The distinction is not considered to be a part of Standard Dutch and is not recognised in educational materials, but it still occurs in many local varieties, such as Antwerpian, [[Limburgish]], [[West Flemish]] and [[Zeelandic]]. In those varieties, the sharp-long vowels are often opening diphthongs such as {{IPA|[ɪə, ʊə]}}, and the soft-long vowels are either plain monophthongs {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|eː}}, {{IPAplink|oː}}]}} or slightly closing {{IPA|[eɪ, oʊ]}}.

====Open vowels====
In Northern Standard Dutch and some other accents, {{IPA|/ɑ, aː/}} are realised and so the former is a back vowel {{IPAblink|ɑ}}, and the latter is central {{IPAblink|äː}} or front {{IPAblink|aː}}. In Belgian Standard Dutch {{IPA|/aː/}} is also central or front, but {{IPA|/ɑ/}} may be central {{IPAblink|ä}}, instead of back {{IPAblink|ɑ}}, and so may have the same backness as {{IPA|/aː/}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=104, 128, 132–133}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}

Other accents may have different realisations:
* Many accents (Amsterdam, Utrecht, Antwerp) realise the pair with 'inverted' backness and so {{IPA|/ɑ/}} is central {{IPAblink|ä}} (or, in the case of Utrecht, even front {{IPAblink|a}}), and {{IPA|/aː/}} is closer to cardinal {{IPAblink|ɑː}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=131, 133}}
* Outside the Randstad, fronting of {{IPA|/ɑ/}} to central {{IPAblink|ä}} is very common. On the other hand, in Rotterdam and Leiden, the short {{IPA|/ɑ/}} sounds even ''darker'' than the Standard Northern realisation by being realised as a fully back and raised open vowel, unrounded {{IPAblink|ɑ|ɑ̝}} or rounded {{IPAblink|ɒ|ɒ̝}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=132}}
* In Groningen, {{IPA|/aː/}} tends to be particularly front and similar to the quality of the cardinal vowel {{IPAblink|aː}}, but in The Hague and the affected Standard Northern accent, {{IPA|/aː/}} may be raised and fronted to {{IPAblink|æː}}, particularly before {{IPA|/r/}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=133}}

Before {{IPA|/r/}}, {{IPA|/ɑ/}} is typically a slight centering diphthong with a centralised first element ({{IPA|[ɐə̆]}}) in Northern Standard Dutch and in the Randstad,{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=130}} e.g. ''nar'' {{Audio-IPA|Nl-nar.ogg|[nɐə̆ʀ]}} ('fool').

===Diphthongs===
[[Image:Dutch diphthong chart.svg|thumb|upright=1.13|[[Diphthongs]] of Northern Standard Dutch, from {{Harvcoltxt|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}|class=skin-invert-image]]
[[Image:Belgian Dutch diphthongs chart.svg|thumb|upright=1.13|Diphthongs of Belgian Standard Dutch, from {{Harvcoltxt|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}|class=skin-invert-image]]
[[Image:Dutch tense backing diphthongs on a vowel chart.svg|thumb|upright=1.13|Dutch tense backing diphthongs, from {{Harvcoltxt|Collins|Mees|2003|p=137}}|class=skin-invert-image]]
[[Image:Dutch tense fronting diphthongs on a vowel chart.svg|thumb|upright=1.13|Dutch tense fronting diphthongs, from {{Harvcoltxt|Collins|Mees|2003|p=137}}|class=skin-invert-image]]
Dutch also has several diphthongs, but only three of them are unquestionably phonemic.{{which|date=May 2025}} All three of them end in a non-syllabic close vowel {{IPA|[i̯, y̑, u̯]}} (henceforth written {{IPA|[i, y, u]}} for simplicity), but they may begin with a variety of other vowels.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Booij|1999|pp=4, 6}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=127, 135}}

