* {{IPA|/ɔu/}} has been variously transcribed with {{angbr IPA|ɔu}},<ref>For example by {{Harvtxt|Booij|1999|pp=4, 6}}, {{Harvtxt|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}} and {{Harvb|Gussenhoven|2007|p=340}}.</ref> {{angbr IPA|ɑu}},<ref>For example by {{Harvtxt|Collins|Mees|2003|p=135}} and {{Harvb|Kooij|van Oostendorp|2003|p=28}}.</ref> and {{angbr IPA|ʌu}}.<ref>For example by {{Harvb|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}.</ref>
* The starting points of {{IPA|/ɛi, œy, ɔu/}} tend to be closer ({{IPA|[ɛɪ, œ̈ʏ, ɔ̈ʊ]}}) in Belgian Standard Dutch than in Northern Standard Dutch ({{IPA|[ɛ̞ɪ, œ̞̈ʏ, ʌ̞̈ʊ]}}). In addition, the Belgian Standard Dutch realisation of {{IPA|/ɔu/}} tends to be fully rounded, unlike the typical Northern Standard Dutch realisation. However, Jo Verhoeven reports rather open starting points of the Belgian Standard Dutch variants of {{IPA|/œy, ɔu/}} ({{IPA|[œ̞̈ʏ, ɔ̞̈ʊ]}}) and so the main difference between Belgian and Northern Standard Dutch in this respect may be only in the rounding of the first element of {{IPA|/ɔu/}}, but the fully rounded variant of {{IPA|/ɔu/}} is also used by some Netherlandic speakers, particularly of the older generation. It is also used in most of Belgium, in line with the Belgian Standard Dutch realisation.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=135–136}}
* In conservative Northern Standard Dutch, the starting points of {{IPA|/ɛi, œy, ɔu/}} are open-mid and rounded in the case of the last two vowels: {{IPA|[ɛɪ, œʏ, ɔʊ]}}.{{sfnp|Stroop|1999}}
* The backness of the starting point of the Belgian Standard Dutch realisation of {{IPA|/ɛi/}} has been variously described as front {{IPA|[ɛɪ]}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=135}} and centralised front {{IPA|[ɛ̈ɪ]}}.{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}
* In ''Polder Dutch'', which is spoken in some areas of the Netherlands (especially the Randstad and its surroundings), the starting points of {{IPA|/ɛi, œy, ɔu/}} are further lowered to {{IPA|[aɪ, aʏ, aʊ]}}. That is typically accompanied by the lowering of the starting points of {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} to {{IPA|[ɛɪ, œʏ, ɔʊ]}}. Such realisations have existed in [[Hollandic dialect|Hollandic]] dialects since the 16th century and are now are becoming standard in the Netherlands. They are an example of a [[chain shift]] that is similar to the [[Great Vowel Shift]] in English. According to Jan Stroop, the fully lowered variant of {{IPA|/ɛi/}} is the same as the phonetic diphthong {{IPA|[aːi]}}, making ''bij'' 'at' and ''baai'' 'bay' perfect homophones.{{sfnp|Stroop|1999}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=136}}
* The rounding of the starting point of the Northern Standard Dutch realisation of {{IPA|/œy/}} has been variously described as slight {{IPA|[œ̜ʏ]}}{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|2007|p=340}} and non-existent {{IPA|[ɐ̜ʏ]}}.{{sfnp|Rietveld|van Heuven|2009}} The unrounded variant has also been reported to occur in many other accents such as in Leiden and Rotterdam and in some Belgian speakers.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=136}}
* Phonetically, the endpoints of the native diphthongs are lower and more central than cardinal {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|i}}, {{IPAplink|y}}, {{IPAplink|u}}]}} and more like {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ɪ}}, {{IPAplink|ʏ}}, {{IPAplink|ʊ}}]}} or even {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|e}}, {{IPAplink|ø}}, {{IPAplink|o}}]}} (however, Jo Verhoven reports a rather close ({{IPAblink|i|ï}}) endpoints of the Belgian Standard Dutch variant of {{IPA|/ɛi/}} and so that might be somewhat variable). In Belgian Standard Dutch, the endpoints are shorter than in Northern Standard Dutch, but in both varieties, the glide is an essential part of the articulation. Furthermore, Northern Standard Dutch has no appreciable difference between the endpoints of {{IPA|/ɛi, œy, ɔu/}} and the phonetic diphthongs {{IPA|[eɪ, øʏ, oʊ]}}, with both sets ending in vowels near {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ɪ}}, {{IPAplink|ʏ}}, {{IPAplink|ʊ}}]}}.{{sfnp|Gussenhoven|1999|p=76}}{{sfnp|Verhoeven|2005|p=245}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=127, 135}}
* In some regional varieties of Standard Dutch (Southern, regional Belgian), the endpoints of {{IPA|/ɛi, œy, ɔu/}} are even lower than in Standard Dutch: {{IPA|[ɛe̞, œø̞, ɔo̞ ~ ʌo̞]}}. In the traditional [[The Hague dialect|dialect of The Hague]], they are pure monophthongs {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ɛː}}, {{IPAplink|œː}}, {{IPAplink|ɑː}}]}}. Broad Amsterdam may also monophthongise {{IPA|/ɛi/}} but to {{IPAblink|aː}}. It typically does not merge with {{IPA|/aː/}}, which has a rather back ({{IPAblink|ɑː}}) realisation in Amsterdam.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=133, 136}}

While {{IPA|[eɪ, øʏ, oʊ]}} occur only in Northern Standard Dutch and regional Netherlands Standard Dutch, all varieties of Standard Dutch have phonetic diphthongs {{IPA|[iu, yu, ui, eːu, ɔi, oːi, ɑi, aːi]}}. Phonemically, they are considered to be sequences of {{IPA|/iʋ, yʋ, uj, eːʋ, ɔj, oːj, ɑj, aːj/}} by Geert Booij and as monosyllabic sequences {{IPA|/iu, yu, ui, eːu, oːi, aːi/}} by Beverley Collins and Inger Mees (they do not comment on {{IPA|[ɔi]}} and {{IPA|[ɑi]}}).{{sfnp|Booij|1999|pp=5, 44}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=134–137}} This article adopts the former analysis.

The endpoints of these "long" diphthongs tend to be slightly more central ({{IPA|[ï, ü]}}) than cardinal {{IPA|[i, u]}}. They tend to be higher than the endpoints of the phonemic diphthongs {{IPA|/ɛi, œy, ɔu/}}.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=135–137}}

In Northern Standard Dutch, the second elements of {{IPA|[iu, yu, eːu]}} may be labiodental {{IPA|[iʋ, yʋ, eːʋ]}}, which is especially common in intervocalic positions.{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|p=137}}

In Northern Standard Dutch and regional Netherlands Standard Dutch, the close-mid elements of {{IPA|[eːu, oːi]}} may be subject to the same kind of diphthongisation as {{IPA|/eː, oː/}} and so they may be actually triphthongs with two closing elements {{IPA|[eɪu, oʊi]}} ({{IPA|[eːu]}} can instead be {{IPA|[eɪʋ]}}, a closing diphthong followed by {{IPA|[ʋ]}}). In Rotterdam, {{IPA|[oːi]}} can be phonetically {{IPA|[əʊi]}}, with a central starting point.{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=44}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=112, 136–137}}

{{IPA|[aːi]}} is realised with more prominence on the first element, according to Booij, and with equal prominence on both elements, according to Collins and Mees. Other diphthongs have more prominence on the first element.{{sfnp|Booij|1999|p=44}}{{sfnp|Collins|Mees|2003|pp=136–137}}

